course: dictionary
lesson: 7306
title: A pitta is a pocket of food
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A pitta is a flat, round or oval bread from the eastern Mediterranean and the Middle East. The clue is in the word's meaning: you can open it into a pocket and fill it with food.
explain: «Lunch was a pitta filled with falafel and salad.» The words filled and salad tell you what goes inside. A pitta is the bread that holds the food, not the food itself.
explain: Pita is another spelling of the same word. You may see both on a menu or in a recipe, and they mean the same bread.
fact: Pitta is a noun: one pitta, two pittas.
fact: Pita is another spelling of pitta, and both mean the same thing.
fact: «Pitta» means a flat round or oval bread that can be opened into a pocket and filled.
fact: Pitta bread comes from the eastern Mediterranean and the Middle East.
fact: People fill pitta with falafel, salad, hummus or gyros, and it is often packed in lunchboxes.
exercise: «She opened the pitta and filled it with salad.» What is a pitta? | A tool for opening tins | A cake with icing on top | A small fish from the sea | *A flat bread that can be opened into a pocket
exercise: Ben says a pitta is a kind of pasta. What is a pitta really? | A sweet biscuit | A type of cheese | A thin noodle for soup | *A flat bread from the Middle East
exercise: Which word is another spelling of pitta? | pizza | *pita | pastry | pasty
test: «Pitta puffs up because water inside turns to steam in the hot oven.» What makes a pitta puff up? | It is rolled very thin | Yeast makes it rise like a cake | It is folded in half | *Water inside turns to steam
test: Where does pitta bread come from? | The Australian outback | *The eastern Mediterranean and the Middle East | Central London | Northern Scotland
exam: Which sentence best explains why a pitta has a pocket? | It is folded in half after baking | Someone sews the edges together with thread | It is made from a pocket-shaped dough | *Water inside turns to steam in a hot oven, so the bread puffs up
